# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_django_positional_createmodel.py" "DJANGO-POSITIONAL-CREATEMODEL GATE: PASS" "Django positional CreateModel mints a table node (62% of real django migrations were missed)" "Django migrations that create a model with the POSITIONAL form CreateModel(\"Name\", [...]) now mint a table node, like the keyword form CreateModel(name=\"Name\", ...) already did, so those migrations couple via the shared table to the models that query it instead of falsely clearing the collision." "Adds a sibling regex _ORM_CREATEMODEL_POS_RE to _cg_schema, consulted at the same call site as the keyword-form _ORM_CREATEMODEL_RE, so Django migrations written in the POSITIONAL form CreateModel(\"Order\", [...]) mint a table node. The gap: _ORM_CREATEMODEL_RE matched ONLY the keyword form CreateModel(name=\"Order\", ...). Django allows both; its CreateModel signature is (name, fields, ...), so the first positional argument is the model name. MEASURED on the real django repo: 43 of 69 (62%) CreateModel calls use the positional form and were MISSED, so those migration files never minted a table node and could not couple via the shared table to the model files that query that table, and Veripsa falsely cleared a real migration to model collision. Confirmed example: tests/migrations/test_migrations/0001_initial.py creates Author and Tribble positionally; the extractor returned only tribble (caught via a separate AddField model_name path) and missed Author. WHAT THIS GATE PROVES (hermetic, no DB, no Postgres, no network, no clone, tiny inline string fixtures): (1) positional CreateModel(\"Author\", [...]) mints the table author; (2) keyword CreateModel(name=\"Order\", ...) STILL mints order -- no regression; (3) PRECISION: a non-model string deeper in the argument list (a field name) does NOT mint a spurious table -- the capture is the first positional argument only, anchored from the open paren to the first token; (4) the realistic django example with Author and Tribble both positional yields BOTH tables; (5) DEDUP: the same model name written in both keyword and positional forms yields exactly one normalized table (_norm_table lowercases, so both forms collapse identically); (6) PRECISION: a variable (non-string) first argument does NOT match -- string-literal anchored. RECALL-SAFE and additive: pure recall gain, the keyword-form behavior is unchanged, and only the model NAME (an identifier) is ever extracted -- content-free, no code bodies." 16
